  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAs the Bermuda High strengthens and moves a little westward in\nthe Atlantic, this will prep the set-up for the rest of the\nweek's weather pattern. Although mainly dry conditions are\nexpected today, a few weak shortwaves riding along the Bermuda\nHigh could push up from the south, bringing chances for showers\nand weak storms into southern areas of NE PA going into the\nevening hours. For the daytime, high temperatures in the low to\nupper 80s can be expected today, with partly to mostly cloudy\nskies.\n\nHeading into Thursday, a longwave trough draped over Canada will\nslowly propagate eastward, allowing for small embedded\nshortwaves to move into the area over the course of a few days\nspanning from Thursday into the weekend. With this set-up, the \ninfluence from the strengthened Bermuda High will allow for \nincreased moisture to be advected into the area, with PWATs \nbetween 1.5 and 2.0 inches through the second half of the week, \nas well as ample instability, especially for Friday, where \nMLCAPE values rising into the 1500-2000 J/kg range for Thursday. \nAlthough ample moisture and instability are forecasted to be \nreadily available on Thursday for storm development, two \ningredients may hinder the ability for severe storms to develop.\nShear is forecasted in the 20-30 knot range, and this pattern \nlacks a strong forcing mechanism for the majority of the second \nhalf of the week. There is one timeframe that we're monitoring \nthe potential for severe weather, which would be Thursday \nafternoon into evening. With moisture and instability present, a\nmore robust shortwave moves into the area Thursday afternoon, \nwhich could be the forcing necessary to spark off some strong to\nsevere storms. Currently, a Marginal Risk for severe weather is\nforecasted over our area from the Storm Prediction Center, with\nthe main threat being strong to damaging winds, as this pattern\nlacks support for the risk of other severe threats.\n\nGoing into Friday, a slow-moving, quasi-stationary front\napproaches, but is expected to weaken quite a bit as it comes\ninto the area. The weekend looks quite showery with chances for\nafternoon storms as the trough in Canada deepens and propagates\neastward a bit more, continuing to push shortwaves into the\narea. Temperatures and dewpoints increase through the second\nhalf of the week, with heat indices increasing into the 90s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe longwave trough over Canada continues into early next week,\nproviding a semi-zonal flow pattern with some embedded\nshortwaves that bring chances for scattered showers and\nafternoon storms for a good portion of early next week. Warm and\nhumid weather is also expected to continue.",
